{"records":[{"id":"bsjc7f3hck","title":"STING-dependent paracriny shapes apoptotic priming of breast tumors in response to  anti-mitotic treatment. Lohard et al.","description":"A fascinating but uncharacterized action of antimitotic chemotherapy is to collectively prime cancer cells to apoptotic mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization (MOMP), while impacting only on cycling cell subsets. Here, we show that a proapoptotic secretory phenotype is induced by activation of cGAS/STING in cancer cells that are hit by antimitotic treatment, accumulate micronuclei and maintain mitochondrial integrity despite intrinsic apoptotic pressure. Organotypic cultures of primary human breast tumors and patient-derived xenografts sensitive to paclitaxel exhibit gene expression signatures typical of type I IFN and TNFα exposure. These cytokines induced by cGAS/STING activation trigger NOXA expression in neighboring cells and render them acutely sensitive to BCL-xL inhibition. cGAS/STING-dependent apoptotic effects are required for paclitaxel response in vivo, and they are amplified by sequential, but not synchronous, administration of BH3 mimetics. Thus anti-mitotic agents propagate apoptotic priming across heterogeneously sensitive cancer cells through cytosolic DNA sensing pathway-dependent extracellular signals, exploitable by delayed MOMP targeting.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-14T14:20:01.824Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bsjc7f3hck","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bsjc7f3hck"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Steven","last_name":"Lohard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fabien","last_name":"Gautier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laurent","last_name":"David","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sophie","last_name":"Barillé-Nion","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xzf382pby9","title":"Adjuvant Ketorolac and Dexamethasone + Hypoxic Human Retinal Pigment Epithelium (RPE) and Endothelial Cells (HREC)","description":"This dataset contains gene and protein expression levels evaluated in human retinal pigment epithelial (RPE) cells and human retinal endothelial cells (HRECs) cultured under hypoxic conditions. The study investigated the hypothesis that adjunctive anti-inflammatory therapies (ketorolac and dexamethasone) can modulate the inflammatory, apoptotic, angiogenic, and fibrotic signaling pathways altered during anti-VEGF (bevacizumab) treatment.\n\nThe SPSS data file (.sav) includes cell viability measurements and relative mRNA/protein expression levels for key targets (including IL-1β, IL-6, BCL2, BAX, TGF-β, and VEGF-A) across different treatment groups: Control, Bevacizumab (312.5 µg/mL) alone, and Bevacizumab combined with Ketorolac (375 µg/mL) and/or Dexamethasone (50 µg/mL). Key findings documented in this dataset show that adjuvant therapies significantly attenuated pro-inflammatory cytokine expression (IL-1β, IL-6) and modulated apoptotic ratios (BCL2/BAX) and fibrotic growth factors (TGF-β, VEGF-A) in a cell-type-specific manner. This data is intended for researchers studying retinal cell biology, anti-VEGF pharmacology, and ocular drug interactions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-27T09:35:03.228Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xzf382pby9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xzf382pby9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fatemeh","last_name":"Sanie-Jahromi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Expression","Protein Expression"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2wv3645ktf","title":"An Epigenetic Program Dictates MYC Amplification Dynamics that Promote Transient, Extrachromosomal and Inherited Genomic Events","description":"SUMMARY (147 words)\nMYC amplification is common and often extrachromosomal (ecDNA) in cancer. In order to define how MYC DNA copy gains arises from diploid genomes, we assessed whether epigenetic enzymes directly control focal MYC over-replication and amplification. We demonstrate that H3K4 methylation primes MYC for amplification by the H3K9/K36 tri-demethylase KDM4C, while the H3K36 tri-methyltransferase SETD2 restrains copy gains. These relationships are also observed in tumor datasets. SETD2 loss or inhibition promotes recruitment of catalytically active KDM4C, which initiates and drives stepwise MYC copy gains. Suppressing apoptosis or losing TP53 accelerates the emergence of higher-level MYC amplification events, including extrachromosomal circular ecDNAs (eccDNAs) and inherited copy gains. Non-transformed cells with these alterations are tumorigenic. Furthermore, KDM4C inhibition suppresses MYC amplification. These data define a chromatin-apoptotic axis directly controlling the initiation and progression of MYC amplification from diploid to hyper-amplification, while identifying biomarkers and therapeutic targets to constrain MYC-amplified tumors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-30T09:12:59.652Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2wv3645ktf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2wv3645ktf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"Ferman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Johnathan","last_name":"Whetstine","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R35GM144131"}],"categories":["Gene Amplification","Cancer Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dhbprzxh2","title":"Part1_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase (FLT)3-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:09:36.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dhbprzxh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dhbprzxh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4m2d88hgz","title":"Part2_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:18.026Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4m2d88hgz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4m2d88hgz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xzjmk67vnc","title":"Part3_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:45.533Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xzjmk67vnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xzjmk67vnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fvzsmrvhps","title":"Data_set Sevoflurane anesthesia modulates hippocampal miRNAs and apoptosis-related gene expression following perinatal asphyxia in rats","description":"Data set that supports the findings from the article entitled Sevoflurane anesthesia modulates hippocampal miRNAs and apoptosis-related gene expression following perinatal asphyxia in rats.\nPerinatal asphyxia (PA) is a leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, with potentially severe neurodevelopmental consequences. General anesthesia (GA) is commonly used in pediatric care, but its impact on the immature brain affected by PA remains poorly understood. Epigenetic mechanisms, particularly microRNAs (miRNAs), may mediate these effects. We aimed to investigate specific miRNAs expression in the immature brain following PA and/or GA and to assess the balance between pro- and anti-apoptotic pathways.\nWistar rats were exposed to 90 minutes of asphyxia or normoxia on postnatal day (PND) 6, and to 120 minutes of sevoflurane anaesthesia or normoxia on PND-15. A total of 62 animals were included in the study; two died during the exposure procedures. Hippocampal tissue was harvested from each of the 4 groups (15 animals/group), and the expression of miRNAs of interest, as well as MAPK1, MAPK3, TP53, CREB1, and BCL2 genes, was assessed by qRT-PCR. The groups were: the C (control) group, the PA (perinatal asphyxia) group, the A (anesthesia) group, and the PAA (perinatal asphyxia and anesthesia) group.\nThe measurements showed an overall low expression of miR_15a_3p, miR_16_3p, miR_34a_3p, and miR_146_3p and higher levels of miR_124_5p and miR_132_5p in the hippocampal tissue among groups. GA significantly increased miRNA levels regardless of PA exposure. GA also downregulated the anti-apoptotic regulators CREB1 and BCL-2, with no significant changes in MAPK1/3 or TP53 expression.\nOur findings indicate CREB-dependent transcriptional dysregulation after GA and regardless of prior PA exposure. Furthermore, GA was associated with epigenetic alterations, suggesting that the observed long-term molecular changes were primarily related to anesthetic exposure rather than prior PA\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-12T16:37:44.933Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fvzsmrvhps","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fvzsmrvhps"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isac","last_name":"Sebastian","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Anesthesia Procedure","Cellular Neuroscience"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z463p2gy9y","title":"Autoimmunity versus Apoptosis in some cases of Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia","description":"Introduce: Apoptosis, with roll in the scheduling of cell life, is a normal process different from cell necrosis, which occurs due\nto physical or chemical aggression. The decision to activate a suicide process is made based on intrinsic or extrinsic apoptotic\nmessages. The extrinsic ways which inducer apoptosis are ligands and cytokines for death receptors, (DR-Death receptors), being\non each cell surface. The intrinsic ways which inducer apoptosis come from the mitochondria or nucleus. Proapoptotic signals,\nwhich promote or cause apoptosis, participate in a cascade that leads to the culmination cleavage of a set of proteins, resulting in\nthe disassembly of the cells for apoptosis.\nAim: The scope of this review is to emphasise that massive apoptosis overloading the phagocytic capacity can trigger an\nautoimmune reaction through the presentation of nucleosomes to the immune system.\nMethods: A large cohort study of cases with the primary Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia, (CLL), examined samples from more\nthan 100 patients for response to MDM2 protein inhibition and found a direct correlation between wild-type P53 status and\nMDM2 inhibitor, Nutlin-3, which induced a cytotoxicity across various CLL subtypes. In experimental models, disrupting the MDM2–p53 protein interaction restored p53 function and sensitized tumours to chemotherapy or radiotherapy. This response was not presented in malignant B cells lymphocytes having the expression of\nZAP-70 receptor membrane, the expression of CD38 receptor with un-mutated immunoglobulin variable genes and with IGHV\nand mono-allelic ATM gene loss.\nResults: Pro-apoptotic signals apoptosis receptors, such as TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand, (TRAIL), Tumour Necrosis\nFactor, (TNF) and Fas receptor, also known as APO-1 or CD95 receptors are the key adaptor proteins transmitting apoptotic\nsignals mediated by the main death receptors, (DRs), known to induce and autophagy process.\nApoptosis is characterized by the apoptotic pathways, starting from the activation of the death receptor, (DRs), which is followed by a downstream signalling cascade including the involvement of mitochondria, subsequent caspase activation and DNA cleavage. However, pro-apoptotic signals apoptosis receptors, such as TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand, (TRAIL), Tumour Necrosis Factor, (TNF) and Fas receptor, also known as APO-1 or CD95 receptors are the key adaptor proteins transmitting apoptotic signals mediated by the main death receptors,\n(DRs). Pro-apoptotic signals, which promote or cause apoptosis, participate in a cascade and leads to the culmination cleavage ogene, which by its product, the p53 protein, inhibits the\nNk-beta nuclear factor.  Mutations of the P53 gene will lead to insufficient action of the p53 protein and trigger\nautoimmunity. Auto-reactive B and T cells, which escaped from natural apoptosis, might represent an additional necessary condition.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-17T13:26:56.933Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z463p2gy9y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z463p2gy9y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"AURELIAN","last_name":"UDRISTIOIU","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01q7jq182","name":"Unitatea Executiva Pentru Finantarea Invatamantului Superior a Cercetarii Dezvoltarii si Inovarii","grant_id":"U-1900-063Y4656"}],"categories":["Oncogenomics","Hematopathology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gwjh3w6ztm","title":"A cellular taxonomy of human normal bone marrow (NBM) and AML ","description":"To generate a cellular taxonomy of the human NBM and AML, representing rare HSPCs, immune cells, neoplastic cells and stromal niche populations, allowing assessment of their cellular diversity, and predicted intercellular signaling, we performed single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) on viably frozen BM aspirates from four healthy donors and 6 AML patients with NPM1 mutation at diagnosis.\nReference: A Single-Cell Taxonomy Predicts Inflammatory Niche Remodeling to Drive Tissue Failure and Outcome in Human AML; doi: 10.1158/2643-3230.BCD-23-0043.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-20T15:19:12.153Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gwjh3w6ztm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gwjh3w6ztm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lanpeng","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Bone Marrow Stromal Cell","Single-Cell RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143618,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":152,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143618,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142823,"restricted":71,"embargoed":724},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2359},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1389},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":699},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":483},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":395},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":341},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140947},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1915},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":141},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":901},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":836},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":539},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":410},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":373}]}}